<<~ ahu slot=`#/entry`>>

! Mandate → Activity → Appraisal

''The agency cut.'' A human, an AI process, a vessel, and an outcome may all participate in one act without becoming one identity. This membrane names the relations so the causal graph carries the distinction.

''Mandate'' names the content-addressed intent an actor authors or delegates. ''Activity'' names one concrete execution attempt. ''Result'' names the entity the activity generates, including a receipt for an external effect. ''Delegation'' relates a responsible actor to a bounded delegate for a named activity. ''Attestation'' names an integrity-bound claim about a subject and activity. ''Appraisal'' names this causal island's local decision over the evidence and capabilities it can presently hold.

The mandate does not become the activity. An attended spirit's act reads as the operator's own, with the Mask as authorship; the AI process does not become the human author. A signature proves who made an attestation; it does not by itself prove permission or pono. Local appraisal decides.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/worldline-boundary`>>

!! Worldline boundary

A tasked spirit consists of an AI, its Mask and the vessel's seat. The seat belongs to the vessel. In attended work, the operator directs the spirit, whose act reads as the operator's own, with the Mask as authorship; under a standing grant, the spirit drafts without a person in the loop. The vessel's own key serves as the spirit's system identity at every boundary. A Mask pins to no key or identifier and records no identity. The derived lineage-path handle, attribution DAG and per-handle clock address and recall the spirit's work and record who drove whom; they grant no identity. The operator answers for the spirit's acts. No separate key, DID, fingerprint or signature belongs to a spirit.

The boundary record carries the parent mandate, activity, result, delegation relation and local appraisal. Re-delegation stays closed by default and must carry an explicit attenuating grant; a child never widens the parent's envelope.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/appraisal`>>

!! Local appraisal runs on a gradient

Every vessel MAY preserve the whole evidence graph while returning a bounded local verdict:

* ''accepted'' — the evidence and held capabilities satisfy local policy;
* ''deferred'' — the evidence can support appraisal, but a human or later local ceremony must decide;
* ''unsettled'' — admissible concurrent heads contradict one another;
* ''unavailable'' — a required parent, authority, or evidence stands absent;
* ''rejected'' — a subject, signature, capability, or relation fails its structural gate.

These stand as local appraisals, never a global present. Arrival order, wall-clock freshness, schedule order, pet-name, persona, and vessel class MUST NOT choose among causal heads.

''Schedule law.'' A scheduler triggers an activity against a fixed mandate CID. At execution the vessel re-appraises that mandate against its locally visible causal frontier and held cap stack. A schedule never means ''latest'', and a timer never grants authority.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/scenario-harness`>>

!! Red-first ScenarioCast guidance

`ScenarioPresence` describes who and what caps stand in the scenario. It does not stand in for an activity. The harness SHOULD compose these pure records:

```
ScenarioPresence   → actor + vessel caps + descriptive hats
ScenarioMandate    → authored/delegated intent entity
ScenarioActivity   → one execution with causal parents
ScenarioResult     → generated subject or external-effect receipt
ScenarioAttestation→ issuer-bound claim about that subject
ScenarioAppraisal  → local accepted/deferred/unsettled/unavailable/rejected
```

An AI process enters the cast as its own actor descriptor when it crosses the explicit-process boundary; it never serves merely as a human `AI` hat. Tests MUST prove that labels do not mint authority, a result cannot use an absent mandate parent, a valid signature on the wrong subject reads rejected, concurrent contradictory activities remain unsettled in either arrival order, and re-delegation cannot widen capabilities.

The fiction failure modes serve as test names, not authority: False Archive tests record/inference separation; Approval-Rot tests contrary evidence; Unsanctioned Expansion tests mandate scope; Context-Fade tests fixed constraints under long runs; Signal-Blur tests epistemic labels; Persona-Capture tests that ritual framing cannot authorize an action.

<<~/ahu>>

